### 1. Java – HashMap (OpenJDK)

Java's HashMap primarily uses separate chaining
to handle hash collisions.

Entries that map to the same bucket are stored in
a linked structure. If too many collisions occur in
one bucket, HashMap can convert that bucket into
a balanced tree.

Java's default load factor is 0.75, meaning the
table is generally resized when the number of
entries exceeds 75% of its capacity.

I think Java uses this approach because separate
chaining is flexible and makes collision handling
straightforward. The tree-based optimization also
helps prevent extremely long lookup times when
many keys collide.

### 2. Python – Dictionary (CPython)

Python's dictionary uses open addressing to handle
collisions. Instead of storing linked lists in
individual buckets, it searches for another
available position within its table.

CPython uses a probing algorithm that incorporates
additional bits from the hash value to determine
where to search next.

Its compact dictionary representation also separates
the index structure from the stored entries.

This helps reduce memory overhead while supporting
efficient lookups and preserving insertion order.

I think Python uses this design because dictionaries
are used extensively throughout the language.
Reducing memory consumption and keeping lookups fast
can improve the performance of many Python programs.

### 3. Google Abseil – Swiss Tables

Google's Abseil library provides hash table
implementations such as absl::flat_hash_map.

Swiss Tables use open addressing and store additional
metadata in control bytes.

These control bytes help identify possible matches
without needing to compare every key individually.

The implementation can use SIMD instructions to
examine multiple control bytes efficiently.

Abseil's flat hash tables generally allow a maximum
load factor of 87.5%.

I think this approach is useful because modern
processors benefit from accessing nearby memory
locations. The compact layout and metadata-based
searching can make lookups very efficient.

## Comparison

| Implementation | Collision Handling | Main Optimization |
|---|---|---|
| Java HashMap | Separate chaining | Tree-based buckets for heavy collisions |
| Python Dictionary | Open addressing | Compact storage and probing |
| Abseil Swiss Tables | Open addressing | Control bytes and SIMD-friendly lookups |
